== Foundation Models for Segmentation

SAM's modular design, a frozen ViT encoder paired with a lightweight mask decoder and a flexible prompt interface, has spawned a family of derivative models that validate the architectural assumptions underlying our approach. SAM-HQ @ke2023samhq adds a learnable high-quality output token to the decoder that refines boundary predictions while leaving the encoder frozen. EfficientSAM @xiong2024efficientsam and MobileSAM @zhang2023mobilesam distill SAM's encoder into ViT-Small and TinyViT backbones respectively, confirming that the decoder functions independently of encoder scale. FastSAM @zhao2023fastsam replaces the transformer encoder entirely with YOLOv8-seg, achieving a 50-fold speedup. These efficiency-oriented works reinforce a central premise of our method: because the encoder can be frozen, compressed, or swapped, a learned prompt policy operating through the prompt interface is architecture-agnostic and forward-compatible with future SAM variants.

A parallel line of research explores alternative prompting paradigms. SegGPT @wang2023seggpt frames segmentation as in-context learning through masked image modeling, where visual examples replace spatial prompts. SEEM @zou2023seem unifies clicks, boxes, text, and referring regions into a single multi-modal prompt framework. Grounding DINO @liu2024groundingdino provides open-set text-driven detection that identifies what to segment, while Grounded-SAM @ren2024groundedsam assembles it with SAM for end-to-end text-to-mask generation. Semantic-SAM @li2024semanticsam addresses granularity ambiguity by producing multi-granularity masks from a single click, a limitation our decomposition strategy also targets. Collectively, these models demonstrate that SAM's prompt interface is the natural control surface for downstream systems, and our work exploits this insight by treating prompt placement itself as a learnable policy.
